  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Cordyceps tenuipes is a rare entomopathogenic fungus in the Cordycipitaceae family that parasitizes insects, primarily in New England forests. This species is notable for its delicate, thread-like fruiting bodies that emerge from infected insect hosts.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Thin, Filiform Fruiting Bodies",
      "description": "Characterized by extremely slender, hair-like or thread-like stalks (hence 'tenuipes,' meaning thin-footed) that are distinctly thinner than most other Cordyceps species",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Host Emergence Pattern",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies emerge from infected insects, typically visible as delicate stalks protruding from the mummified remains of the host organism",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Perithecial Structure",
      "description": "The fertile head of the fruiting body contains small, flask-shaped perithecia that contain the microscopic spores",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Cordyceps tenuipes is found in moist, shaded forests and woodlands of New England, where it parasitizes insects in leaf litter and decomposing organic matter.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Like other Cordyceps species, C. tenuipes exhibits a remarkable parasitic strategy where the fungus controls the host insect's behavior before killing it, positioning the host in an optimal location for spore dispersal.",
